 "verdict": "FAMILY SCALE LADDER on The Pile. 96 starts x 2 seeds, CPU float32, estimator and class rule imported unchanged; existing cells reused, not re-measured.  Pythia across 4 scales (70M-1000M): 70M funnel phi=0.802; 160M funnel phi=0.432; 410M funnel phi=0.458; 1000M funnel phi=0.365 -- UNIFORM.  GPT-Neo across 3 scales (125M-2700M): 125M none phi=0.052; 1300M none phi=0.000; 2700M none phi=0.036 -- UNIFORM.  BOTH LADDERS ARE UNIFORM: Pythia funnels at every scale tested and GPT-Neo at none. F178's split is therefore a stable property of the families across roughly an order of magnitude in size, not an artefact of two checkpoints. KH, registered before the run and binding whatever the ladders did: this does NOT settle whether the Pythia recipe is idiosyncratic. A uniform ladder shows the split is stable; it does not show it is architectural, recipe-driven, or general. Settling that needs a second funnel from a different family at fixed corpus, and no affordable candidate exists -- GPT-J-6B is ~24GB in float32 on a 16GB machine. REFUSALS: no p-value; no claim about WHAT differs between the families; no extrapolation beyond the scales tested.",
